{
  // Impact Soundworks Shreddage 3 Hydra, hosted in Kontakt Player.
  //
  // CALIBRATED 2026-09-07, from inside the host. The probe still cannot reach
  // it - Shreddage is a Kontakt library rather than a plugin, so loading Kontakt
  // headlessly gives an empty instance, the same wall as IRON 2 in Player mode -
  // so this was done by ear at the keyboard, which is the only way available.
  //
  // THE SILENT LOW REGISTER: FOUND, 2026-09-11. It was never Ghostband, and it
  // was never the range.
  //
  // Kontakt's instrument options, DFD tab, "Background Loading": the box marked
  // "Allow instant playback for samples which are not loaded yet" was UNCHECKED.
  // With it off Kontakt refuses to sound a sample that is not resident yet - it
  // gives SILENCE rather than streaming it from disk. The low strings are the
  // least-used zones, so they are the ones not resident on a cold instance.
  //
  // Every observation collected over five sessions falls out of that one box:
  // silent below 40 when played by hand; audible during calibration and quiet
  // afterwards; 27 and 28 sounding when tested but silent on a cold open; note
  // 88 playing cold while 27 did not (the high zone was resident, the low one
  // was not); and working immediately after any note was played inside
  // Shreddage's own interface, which is what forced the load.
  //
  // Five theories died on the way, each by measurement: a six-string library
  // (the owner confirmed eight), a keyswitch collision (Shreddage's switches sit
  // at 12-23 and 108/114), an articulation (154 of 166 sub-40 notes fire under
  // plain Sustain), legato reach (Calibrate can hold a note under another now,
  // and both sound), and Ghostband auditioning the wrong note (all 29 steps
  // send exactly what they display - pinned).
  //
  // The lesson is that all five were about WHICH NOTE, because the report was
  // phrased as a range. The evidence that cracked it was about WHEN, and it
  // surfaced only when the owner described what he DID rather than what he
  // concluded.
  //
  // The DFD PRELOAD BUFFER is a different setting and does not fix this. It
  // governs how much of an already-loaded sample is held in RAM; a purged zone
  // is not partly resident, it is absent, and no buffer size reaches it. But
  // changing that buffer explains the intermittency that made this so hard to
  // pin down: Kontakt's own note says every change to it forces a full reload of
  // the instrument's samples, so each time it was adjusted the low register
  // worked for a while and then went quiet again.
  //
  // ---- two settings confirmed from the same screenshots ----
  //
  // Controller tab, "Accept standard controllers for Volume (#7) and Pan (#10)"
  // is UNCHECKED. Shreddage genuinely ignores CC 7, which is why a stale CC 7
  // once muted it and why volume has to go through a learned controller.
  //
  // Controller tab, "Accept All Notes Off (#123) and All Sounds Off (#120)" is
  // UNCHECKED. Ghostband releases every note by name at a section jump rather
  // than trusting All Notes Off; on this rig that is not caution, it is the only
  // thing standing between a jump and a permanently hanging note.
  //
  // Voice Handling shows a 32-voice limit with "Oldest" stealing. Nothing has
  // hit it, but it is the first place to look if notes ever cut out in a dense
  // passage with long release tails.
  //
  // Below roughly 40 the instrument is silent when played by hand. It sounded
  // during calibration and went quiet again afterwards, with nothing changed.
  // Performance Style was ruled out by test - it has been Mono Lead (Mid/High)
  // throughout, and switching to Standard did not bring the notes back.
  // Unexplained. If it is ever worth chasing, the thing left to suspect is the
  // loaded patch's own string configuration or tuning: an eight string playing
  // as six has no F#1 or B1, which is exactly this symptom.
  //
  // AND IT DOES MATTER, contrary to what was written here an hour ago.
  //
  // That claim - "Ghostband never sends a note that low" - was made from two
  // songs. thrash plays 58-87 and blues 62-86, so it looked settled. Measured
  // across all eleven, six of them write into the disputed region:
  //
  //     alt-rock 30    punk 35    ballad 36    prog 37    metal 38    emo 38
  //
  // Two samples, a confident generalisation, and the six counterexamples were
  // one command away. This is the same error as "the solo plays 34 to 87
  // without a hole" - reasoning about the whole from the part that was easy to
  // look at.
  //
  // lowest_note is 27 - re-measured at the keyboard on 2026-09-11, one below the
  // 28 recorded before - and that is the owner's decision, not an oversight.
  //
  // It was briefly clamped to 40 on the reasoning that a lead guitar has no
  // business below E2 and the generator would lose nothing. The owner set it
  // back: "the gtr 2 has been calibrated to the actual lowest note, not 40 -
  // this is on purpose, but if solos play at 40 and above, it doesn't matter
  // anyway." Which is right on both counts. The file should record what the
  // instrument is, and the clamp was solving a problem the arrangement does not
  // actually have.
  //
  // So if the silent low register ever becomes audible in a song, the fix is to
  // find out why it is silent, not to narrow the number that describes it.
  //
  // THE RANGE. Hydra is an EIGHT string, tuned F#1 B1 E2 A2 D3 G3 B3 E4 at
  // concert pitch, so the reasoning went: F#1 is 30, therefore 30 is the
  // bottom. See chord_zone below for how that turned out.
  //
  // It has not been confirmed by ear, but it is bracketed by something that has:
  // Shreddage's own keyswitch block. Kontakt's keyboard is labelled -2 to 8, so
  // C-2 is 0, and the articulation switches sit at 12 to 23 while Picking Mode
  // and Fretting Mode sit at 108 and 114. Anything Ghostband writes between 30
  // and 88 therefore cannot land on a keyswitch, which is the only mistake here
  // that does real damage - a note outside the sampled range is merely silent,
  // a note on a keyswitch changes how everything after it plays.
  //
  // KEYSWITCHES, read off the ARTICULATIONS page. Recorded here because they are
  // certain and worth not having to look up again, NOT because anything uses
  // them yet - see below.
  //
  //    12  C-1   Sustain              (velocity 1-119)
  //    13  C#-1  Mute
  //    14  D-1   Staccato
  //    15  D#-1  Power Chord Sustain
  //    16  E-1   Power Chord Mute
  //    17  F-1   Power Chord Staccato
  //    18  F#-1  Tremolo
  //    19  G-1   Choke
  //    20  G#-1  Tapping
  //    21  A-1   Harmonics
  //    23  B-1   FX
  //
  //    12  C-1   Pinch Harmonics      (same key, velocity 127 only)
  //    12  C-1   Rake                 (same key, velocity 120-126)
  //
  //     0  C-2   Force String
  //    10  Bb-2  Set Hand
  //   108  C7    Picking Mode
  //   114  F#7   Fretting Mode
  //
  // There is a second articulations page not yet read.
  //
  // NOTHING SENDS THESE YET. Ghostband only emits keyswitches for phrase-driven
  // instruments, where the keyswitch is the performance and is held down for the
  // length of it. Shreddage is a notes instrument whose keyswitches choose an
  // articulation alongside the notes - a different mechanism, and one the engine
  // does not have. Until it does, this plays sustains, which is whatever
  // articulation the instrument is left on.

  "name": "Shreddage 3 Hydra (Kontakt)",
  "id": "shreddage3_hydra",
  // Eleven. As the second guitar it cannot share channel 2 with the rhythm
  // guitar, and this is where the rig has it. If you use Shreddage as a song's
  // ONLY guitar, set the GUITAR channel to 11 in Settings, or it will be sent
  // notes on 2 and hear nothing.
  "channel": 11,

  "mode": "notes",

  "needs_verification": false,
  "verification_note": "Range, bends, legato and keyswitches all confirmed by ear at the keyboard, 2026-09-07.",

  // 28 to 88, MEASURED at the keyboard on 2026-09-07. This replaces 30, which
  // was reasoned and wrong.
  //
  // The reasoning was: Hydra is an eight string tuned F#1 B1 E2 A2 D3 G3 B3 E4,
  // F#1 is 30, therefore 30 is the bottom. Sound reasoning from a correct fact,
  // and still two semitones out - the library samples below its own open low
  // string, which no amount of thinking about the tuning would have revealed.
  //
  // Worth remembering how close the wrong number looked to right. The solo
  // plays across 34 to 87 without a hole, and that was offered as evidence the
  // range was correct. It was evidence about what Ghostband WROTE, not about
  // what Shreddage SOUNDED, and those are not the same claim.
  "chord_zone": { "lowest_note": 27, "highest_note": 88 },

  // 119, not 127, and this one matters.
  //
  // Shreddage splits the Sustain keyswitch by velocity: 1-119 is a sustain,
  // 120-126 is a Rake, and 127 is a Pinch Harmonic. They are the same key, so
  // the only thing separating a played note from a rake is how hard it is hit.
  // The first version of this file capped at 120, which means Ghostband's
  // loudest accents - the landing note of every solo phrase, which is
  // deliberately the hardest hit in the line - would have come out as rakes.
  // That would have sounded like the library misbehaving rather than like a
  // number being one too high.
  "velocity_min": 45,
  "velocity_max": 119,

  // ---- articulation keyswitches ----
  //
  // Read off Shreddage's own ARTICULATIONS page, not guessed. A notes
  // instrument's keyswitch chooses HOW the notes it is sent will sound; it is
  // tapped rather than held, because these latch.
  //
  // Mapped to the section feel, which is the same vocabulary the rest of the
  // engine already speaks:
  //
  //   open    12  Sustain              ringing chords
  //   sparse  14  Staccato             short and spaced
  //   muted   13  Mute                 palm muted, the verse voice
  //   driving 15  Power Chord Sustain  the chorus riff
  //   busy    18  Tremolo              tremolo picking
  //   solo    12  Sustain              a lead line wants to ring, and legato
  //                                    needs a sustained note to hammer from
  //
  // "silent" is deliberately unmapped: there is no articulation for not
  // playing, and leaving the last one selected through a rest is correct.
  // ---- WHAT THE WARNING TRIANGLES MEAN, settled 2026-09-07 ----
  //
  // Shreddage compares articulation rules ONE DIMENSION AT A TIME, and flags an
  // overlap in that dimension without noticing that another dimension already
  // separates them. It is informational: "these could collide", not "these do".
  //
  // Worked out from two states rather than from any tooltip - there is none.
  //
  // Twelve triangles, when six articulations moved to CC 40 bands while four
  // others were left on an earlier five-band layout that ran across them.
  // Sustain at 0-20 was the only band nothing reached into, and the only one
  // clean. Overlap in the CC dimension.
  //
  // Three triangles, once those four went back to keyswitches - and on exactly
  // the three still carrying a velocity rule: Sustain 1-119, Harmonics 1-127,
  // Tremolo 1-127. Those ranges overlap each other, and Shreddage says so even
  // though their CC bands and keyswitches make them mutually exclusive.
  // Overlap in the velocity dimension.
  //
  // Same check, two dimensions, both explained. The owner spotted the second
  // one: "bc their velocities overlap... could just be letting us know".
  //
  // CLEARED 2026-09-07. The three velocity rules were deleted and every
  // triangle went with them, which confirms the reading above: it was the
  // overlap between those three ranges and nothing else. None of the three did
  // any work - Ghostband caps this instrument at velocity 119, and the other
  // two spanned the whole range.
  //
  // NOTHING GHOSTBAND USES IS AMBIGUOUS. Every articulation it sends is
  // selected by a CC 40 band that no other articulation claims, and the wire
  // was measured: 64 messages on thrash, no repeats, every gesture restored.

  // ---- ONE CONTROLLER, FIVE VALUE BANDS ----
  //
  // Shreddage's Articulations page takes a MIDI CC rule with a value RANGE, so
  // five articulations fit on one controller rather than needing five. Set in
  // the instrument as Rule 1 of each articulation:
  //
  //   Sustain               CC 40,   0 - 24
  //   Mute                  CC 40,  25 - 49
  //   Staccato              CC 40,  50 - 74
  //   Power Chord Sustain   CC 40,  75 - 99
  //   Tremolo               CC 40, 100 - 127
  //
  // The values below sit in the MIDDLE of each band, so a value that arrives
  // slightly wrong cannot cross a boundary into the neighbouring articulation.
  //
  // CC 40 is clear of everything this rig uses: the modwheel at 1, which drives
  // Shreddage's vibrato, channel volume at 7, and the eleven taught controls at
  // 20 to 30. Kontakt's default for a new rule is CC 1 with a range of 0-127 -
  // both wrong here, and quietly: the modwheel would re-select an articulation
  // on every vibrato move, and a full-width range is true of every value, so
  // that articulation would simply always be on.
  //
  // WHY CONTROLLERS AT ALL. A keyswitch is a note, and a note aimed at the
  // wrong instrument gets PLAYED - which is what happened when IRON 2 received
  // this instrument's switches and treated all of them as music. A controller
  // nothing has learned does nothing at all. That is also why this block can be
  // written before the instrument is set up: until the rules exist in
  // Shreddage, Ghostband sends CC 40 and nothing happens.
  //
  // The keyswitch each one used to use is kept beside its cc. It is a record
  // rather than an instruction - the cc is what gets sent - and those numbers
  // were read off the Articulations page once, which is work worth not redoing.
  "phrases": {
    "open":    { "cc": 40, "value": 10, "keyswitch": 12 },
    "sparse":  14,
    "muted":   13,
    "driving": 15,
    "busy":    18,
    "solo":    { "cc": 40, "value": 10, "keyswitch": 12 },

    // Fills get Sustain too, and this one was missing until the wire was
    // measured: an unmapped feel selects NOTHING, so an answering phrase came
    // out on whatever articulation happened to be latched. Follow a chorus of
    // power chords with a two-note answer and the answer is power chords.
    //
    // Sustain for the same reason the solo has it - an answer wants to ring,
    // and legato needs a sustained note to hammer from.
    "fills":   { "cc": 40, "value": 10, "keyswitch": 12 }
  },

  // ---- PER-NOTE GESTURES ----
  //
  // What a lead player does to ONE note, as opposed to how a whole section is
  // played. Nobody plays a verse in pinch harmonics; you hit one note that way,
  // for emphasis - and doing that or not is most of what separates a guitarist
  // from a keyboard playing guitar samples.
  //
  // DECLARED EMPTY ON PURPOSE, which is the honest state of it.
  //
  // Shreddage holds one articulation at a time and selects it from a single
  // control, so a gesture and a section style compete for the same CC. CC 40 is
  // fully spoken for by the five bands above. Reaching these means re-banding
  // that controller to carry ten rather than five, which is work in the
  // instrument rather than in this file - see the table below.
  //
  // Meanwhile Ghostband asks for gestures and this declares none, so nothing is
  // sent and the solos play exactly as they do today. That is the controller
  // design paying for itself a second time: an unmapped gesture is silence
  // rather than a wrong note.
  //
  // TO TURN THEM ON, re-band CC 40 in Shreddage to these ten and fill in the
  // block below to match:
  //
  //     0 - 12    Sustain                65 -  77   Rake
  //    13 - 25    Mute                   78 -  90   Pinch Harmonics
  //    26 - 38    Staccato               91 - 103   Harmonics
  //    39 - 51    Power Chord Sustain   104 - 116   Tapping
  //    52 - 64    Tremolo               117 - 127   Choke
  //
  // The keyswitches are recorded because they are measured and certain:
  //
  //     rake      12 at key velocity 120-126     pinch  12 at key velocity 127
  //     choke     19                             harmonic  21
  //     tap       20
  //
  // Rake and pinch are unreachable by keyswitch now in any case. They share
  // note 12 with Sustain and are told apart from it by the velocity the KEY is
  // pressed at - and Sustain no longer uses note 12 at all.
  "lead_articulations": {
    "rake":     { "cc": 40, "value":  31, "keyswitch": 12 },
    "pinch":    { "cc": 40, "value":  52, "keyswitch": 12 },
    "harmonic": { "cc": 40, "value":  73, "keyswitch": 21 },
    "tap":      { "cc": 40, "value":  94, "keyswitch": 20 },
    "choke":    { "cc": 40, "value": 116, "keyswitch": 19 }
  },

  "keyswitches_verified": true,

  // 100, and this is not arbitrary.
  //
  // Shreddage splits the Sustain keyswitch by the velocity the KEY is pressed
  // at, exactly as it splits played notes: 1-119 selects Sustain, 120-126
  // selects Rake and 127 selects Pinch Harmonics. Press the articulation switch
  // too hard and the instrument selects a different articulation from the one
  // being asked for.
  "phrase_velocity": 100,

  // A lead line is one note at a time, so there is nothing to spread. Left at
  // zero rather than borrowed from the rhythm guitar profile, where nine ticks
  // of strum is what stops a chord sounding like a keyboard.
  "strum_ticks": 0,

  // Legato, and it needs no keyswitch at all.
  //
  // Shreddage's ARTICULATIONS page 2 lists Legato (Hammer/Pull) with velocity
  // 1-127 and no key against it: it is always armed and fires whenever a new
  // note is taken while the previous one is still sounding. Ghostband could not
  // produce that, because the lead renderer cut every note to end exactly where
  // the next began - so all 1616 notes of a song came out individually picked.
  //
  // Twelve ticks is about ten milliseconds at this tempo. Enough for the script
  // to see the overlap, far too little to hear as two notes at once. Bent notes
  // are excluded automatically; the wheel has to come home before the next note
  // starts.
  //
  // max_leap_semitones matches Shreddage's own hammer/pull range, which the
  // manual says defaults to two semitones - a wider interval will not trigger a
  // hammer-on however much it overlaps, so overlapping one buys nothing and
  // gives a monophonic instrument one more chance to steal its own voice.
  //
  // It matters for a second reason. Shreddage scales legato volume down until a
  // non-legato note is struck ("Realistic Volume", manual p22), so a run in
  // which every note is hammered fades away to nothing. Picking the leaps is
  // what resets it - which is also what a real player does when the hand moves.
  //
  // CONFIRMED BY EAR: the bends are audible and the legato flows, with the
  // settings below applied. What is still only reasoned is the range - the solo
  // plays across 34 to 87 without a hole, which is evidence but not proof that
  // 30 and 88 are the real ends.
  //
  // SETTINGS THIS EXPECTS IN SHREDDAGE, from the manual:
  //   Performance Style = Mono Lead (Mid/High)   "perfect for lead parts";
  //                       Standard plays low on the neck and prefers open strings
  //   Poly Input Latency = 0                     manual p11 recommends very low
  //                       or zero for fast parts, or the fretting jumps around
  //   Pitch Bend Range   = 2                     matches the bend block below
  "legato": { "overlap_ticks": 12, "max_leap_semitones": 2 },

  // Bends. Declared because this is a lead instrument and pitch bend is the
  // articulation Ghostband can send safely - it is a channel message rather
  // than a keyswitch, so the worst case is that nothing happens.
  //
  // range_semitones must match Shreddage's own wheel range. Two is the usual
  // default and what is assumed here; if the bends land short or sharp, that is
  // the number to correct, and Shreddage exposes it in its own interface.
  "bend": { "range_semitones": 2, "reach_semitones": 2, "ticks": 240 },

  // Vibrato, on the modwheel, which is where Shreddage takes it by default
  // (MAIN page, Vibrato Amount, gear icon - Controller: modwheel or aftertouch).
  //
  // The other half of the bend. A guitarist slides up into a note and then
  // shakes it; a held note that just sits there is the clearest sign nobody is
  // holding the instrument. It comes in after the bend has arrived rather than
  // with the attack, because vibrato from the attack sounds like an LFO.
  //
  // 90 rather than 127: full modwheel is a wobble, not a vibrato. Raise
  // Shreddage's own Vibrato Amount if you want more depth per unit of wheel.
  //
  // Set Mode to Fingered on that gear icon for the recorded vibrato samples;
  // Emulated is an LFO on the pitch and sounds like one.
  "vibrato": { "cc": 1, "depth": 90, "ticks": 160 },

  // ---- tone, and the hard limit on it ----
  //
  // Shreddage has TWO layers of controls and only one of them can be reached.
  //
  //   Shreddage Engine (the MAIN page)  - "virtually every knob can be MIDI
  //     learned", manual p7. Right-click the knob, move a CC.
  //
  //   Console (the amps, distortion, cabinets, EQ, delay - everything that
  //     makes a lead tone a lead tone) - CANNOT be automated or MIDI learned
  //     at all. From the Console manual's own FAQ: "The only way to make
  //     Console completely modular was to remove native automation / CC learn
  //     from the controls." The escape hatch it names is a Mod Matrix script,
  //     and Hydra does not have one.
  //
  // So Ghostband cannot switch Shreddage from a clean tone to a lead tone. What
  // it CAN do is push a fixed tone harder, which is what a guitarist does
  // anyway: the amp does not change for the solo, the playing hits it harder.
  //
  //   Volume          MAIN page, top left, under Tone & Signal. It is PRE-FX
  //                   gain, so raising it drives whatever amp is in Console
  //                   harder. This is the gain control, despite its name.
  //   Bite            MAIN page, right hand group. Adds brightness and takes
  //                   out low-mid body. Up for a solo, so the line cuts.
  //   Signal          the pickup selector: neck / neck+bridge / bridge. Neck is
  //                   round and vocal and is where leads live.
  //
  // NOT HERE, and deliberately - neither can be reached:
  //
  //   Multi-Tracking  four on/off toggles, not a knob. The manual says
  //                   "enabling the guitar of choice and disabling any other
  //                   guitars", and right-click MIDI learn does nothing on a
  //                   toggle - "virtually every KNOB" does not cover them.
  //                   Set by hand, once: ONE guitar for a lead patch, because
  //                   a doubled lead smears. It cannot change per section.
  //
  //   a level control  Nothing follows "level" here, so Ghostband falls back
  //                   to CC 7 for this part mix knob. That costs nothing and
  //                   may work outright. If it does not, the next route is
  //                   Kontakt own automation - and Console faders are never
  //                   an option, their CC learn having been removed to make
  //                   Console modular.
  //
  // All four follow "lead" - up, brighter, warmer and single-tracked when this
  // guitar is out front, back to the rhythm setting when it is not.
  //
  // THESE CC NUMBERS ARE ASSIGNED, NOT TAUGHT. Ghostband will send them and
  // Shreddage will ignore them until each knob is right-clicked and learned. A
  // CC nothing has learned does nothing, which is why declaring them is safe
  // where declaring a keyswitch would not be.
  //
  // For the mix knob, see "level" below: Console's faders cannot be learned, so
  // the volume that mix reaches has to be KONTAKT's instrument volume, assigned
  // from Kontakt's Automation tab - drag a MIDI CC onto the slider. That is not
  // a right-click, which is why right-clicking it did nothing.
  // NAMED AFTER THE KNOB YOU RIGHT-CLICK, not after what it does. The first
  // version called these drive/bite/pickup/tracks, and three of those four are
  // not written anywhere on Shreddage's interface - so the Settings list named
  // controls that appeared not to exist.
  //
  // VOLUME FOLLOWS "level", which is a change of mind and worth saying why.
  //
  // It followed "lead" - up when this guitar is out front - which is musically
  // the better use of a pre-FX gain and left NOTHING for the GTR 2 mix knob to
  // reach. The knob then fell back to CC 7, which Kontakt did not answer, so it
  // moved nothing at all. That is this project's own rule broken by its own
  // author: a control that does nothing is worse than no control.
  //
  // A working fader beats automated gain. Bite still follows "lead" and carries
  // most of that gesture anyway - a solo cuts through because it is brighter
  // more than because it is hotter.
  //
  // It is still pre-FX gain rather than an output level, so turning it down
  // makes the guitar cleaner as well as quieter. If that becomes a problem the
  // honest target is KONTAKT's instrument volume: its Automation tab, MIDI tab,
  // drag CC 20 onto the volume slider. That is a drag, not a right-click, which
  // is why right-clicking it did nothing.
  "controls": {
    "volume": { "cc": 20, "follows": "level", "type": "knob", "low": 0, "high": 1 },
    // Already the one control that told a fill from a solo - 19 against 108 -
    // and now it has company rather than doing the whole job alone.
    "bite": { "cc": 21, "follows": "lead", "type": "knob", "low": 0.1, "high": 0.9 },
    // THE PICKUP, and the biggest tonal difference available here.
    //
    // "select" rather than "switch": it has three positions - neck,
    // neck+bridge, bridge - and a switch only has two, so its positions were
    // being ignored and it sat on 0 for entire songs.
    //
    // Following "lead" puts the NECK pickup on an answering part and the BRIDGE
    // on a solo, which is what a guitarist does without thinking about it:
    // round and vocal behind somebody, bright and cutting out front.
    "signal": { "cc": 22, "follows": "lead", "type": "select", "positions": 3, "low": 0, "high": 1 },
    // Pick definition, so it follows how hard the section is being played.
    // "random" meant a quiet verse could get more attack than a full chorus,
    // which is backwards - a player digs in as a song lifts. Never all the way
    // off, because a guitar with no pick attack sounds like a pad.
    "xtra attack": { "cc": 23, "follows": "intensity", "type": "knob", "low": 0.25, "high": 0.9 },
    "pitch bend range": { "cc": 24, "follows": "none", "type": "knob", "low": 0, "high": 1 },
    // A soloist's gesture - two strings bent to the same note - so it belongs
    // to whoever is out front and to nobody else. Zero behind the rhythm
    // guitar, a moderate amount on a solo, capped well short of full: a unison
    // bend on everything stops reading as a flourish and starts reading as
    // tuning trouble.
    "unison bend": { "cc": 25, "follows": "lead", "type": "knob", "low": 0.0, "high": 0.45 },
    // "lead" rather than "random". Random meant the tone wandered from section
    // to section with no relation to what the guitar was doing - which is
    // variety, but not a voice. Tied to the role it becomes one: darker behind
    // the rhythm guitar, brighter when out front.
    "tone": { "cc": 26, "follows": "lead", "type": "knob", "low": 0.2, "high": 0.85 },
    // ---- the four below are SETTINGS, and stay on "none" ----
    //
    // "none" means Ghostband never sends them. Each has one correct value that
    // does not change during a song, so there is nothing for an arrangement to
    // express through them - and every controller this plugin transmits is one
    // more thing a knob sitting in MIDI Learn can latch onto by mistake. That
    // is not hypothetical: Shreddage's Pitch Bend Range learned the mix knob's
    // CC in exactly that way.
    //
    // "fixed" would hold them actively rather than leave them alone. Wrong
    // trade here: it buys protection against somebody nudging a knob, and pays
    // in traffic on a wire that has already caused one wrong mapping. Set these
    // by hand, once.
    //
    //   strumming mode        a lead line is one note at a time; there is
    //                         nothing to strum. Leave it off.
    //   MIDI guitar mode      for playing Shreddage FROM a guitar controller.
    //                         Ghostband is not one.
    //   invert MIDI channels  changes what arrives on which channel - the one
    //                         control in this file that could silently send the
    //                         whole part to the wrong instrument.
    //   anti-repetition       stops the same sample firing twice in a row.
    //                         Worth having ON permanently; set it once in
    //                         Shreddage, it is not an arrangement decision.
    "strumming mode": { "cc": 27, "follows": "none", "type": "switch", "low": 0, "high": 1 },
    "MIDI guitar mode": { "cc": 28, "follows": "none", "type": "switch", "low": 0, "high": 1 },
    "invert MIDI channels": { "cc": 29, "follows": "none", "type": "switch", "low": 0, "high": 1 },
    "anti-repetition": { "cc": 30, "follows": "none", "type": "switch", "low": 0, "high": 1 }
  }
}

